      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+8 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+8 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+8 \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=6{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{1}{|x+3|\\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\sqrt{2}-3$ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{arcsec}\\,u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =6(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
